{"answer_pre_raw":"```json
{
  "answer_pre": "The asymmetry found in each study implies that the risk of relying on a single averaged effect estimate in survey-experiment research on preferences is that different conditions may lead to different outcomes. For example, highly educated women may reject a potential partner if only two deficiencies in socioeconomic traits are present, whereas women without higher education tend to reject based on a wider range of reasons.",
  "evidence_pages": [228, 229]
}
