{"answer_pre_raw":"```json
{
  "answer_pre": "Across the Dutch attribution-migration experiment, the psilocybin communication experiment, and the QCA reanalysis of Chinese mate-preference data, each study demonstrates that a manipulated informational or attributional treatment does not produce a uniform effect across all sub-outcomes or subgroups, and this collectively suggests that the risk of assuming homogeneous treatment effects in survey-experiment research on attitude or preference change is that the results are not uniform across all sub-outcomes or subgroups.",
  "evidence_pages": [228, 229]
}
