{"answer_pre_raw":"{
  "answer_pre": "The collision-avoidance and supervisor-synthesis papers provide conditional guarantees that depend on specific assumptions (e.g., bounded disturbance measurements in the collision-avoidance paper), whereas the switched-system embedding paper offers an unconditional guarantee for bang-bang solutions. This difference arises because the embedding approach reformulates the discrete switching problem into a continuous optimal control problem, enabling the use of established continuous optimization techniques like Pontryagin's Minimum Principle.",
  "evidence_pages": [
    1,
    2
  ]
