{"answer_pre_raw":"```json
{
  "answer_pre": "In the Bayesian SBD model, sparse exact observations are incorporated as hard constraints by conditioning on the joint blur-image conditional distribution, resulting in a Gibbs measure over histories. In contrast, the HMC blur update uses a hybrid scheme that samples the blur from its full conditional distribution using a combination of Gibbs sampling and Hamiltonian Monte Carlo (HMC) updates. The Gibbs approach enforces these constraints by conditioning on the joint blur-image conditional distribution, leading to a Gibbs measure over histories, while the HMC update leverages the cyclic model formulation with separable and stationary correlation matrices to efficiently perform matrix operations in the Fourier domain, allowing for efficient handling of hard constraints.",
  "evidence_pages": [
    "Doc 2",
    "Doc 3"
  ]
}
