{"answer_pre_raw":"```json
{
  "answer_pre": "Both papers justify treating small yet significant coefficients as evidence of a real mechanism by demonstrating that these coefficients are statistically significant after controlling for potential confounders and mediators in their respective settings. In the SPRINT paper, this is achieved through the use of a cross-lagged structural equation model (CL), which accounts for changes in covariates over time, ensuring that the observed effects are not due to time-varying covariates. In the SHARELIFE paper, the use of a latent growth model (LGM) allows for the assessment of how initial differences in socioeconomic status (SES) impact health trajectories over time, showing that even though the coefficients are small, they are significant after accounting for repeated measures of SES and health.",
  "evidence_pages": [
    1,
    2
  ]
