{"answer_pre_raw":"```json
{
  "answer_pre": "In discretization approximation, the concentration of the probability measure onto a simpler object (a discrete measure on a set of support points) preserves the target distribution's means, standard deviations, quantiles, and other summary statistics by approximating them with those of the discrete distribution. In contrast, in irreversible kinetics from Bayesian inference over admissible histories, the MAP history is the one that maximizes the Gibbs-type probability measure over all admissible histories consistent with the observed data. This concentration preserves the most probable path taken by the system, which may not necessarily align with the mean or other summary statistics of the posterior distribution. The difference matters because discretization approximation provides a deterministic, fast, and low-complexity summary of the posterior distribution, while the MAP history from irreversible kinetics captures the most probable evolution path given the data, which can be different from the summary statistics of the full posterior distribution.",
  "evidence_pages": [
    1,
    2,
    3,
     ]
}
