{"answer_pre_raw":"{
  "answer_pre": "In the first paper, the corrective mechanism is spectral decomposition (SVD) applied to Hankel matrices derived from attack data, which enforces binary switching variables. In the second paper, the corrective mechanism is the embedding of binary switching variables into continuous embedded variables, followed by enforcing bang-bang behavior through a penalty term in the cost function. The first paper exploits the rank condition of the Hankel matrices, while the second paper exploits the concavity of the penalty term to enforce binary values.",
  "evidence_pages": [1, 20]
